WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.752
Two original conversations, two different needs:

00:00:03.442 --> 00:00:05.170
Alex: “Why are we changing?

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:09.634
My emails have always worked.” Ask what Alex knows about missed handoffs.

00:00:09.874 --> 00:00:15.037
Show an anonymized, accurate example and explain how the new queue addresses the problem.

00:00:15.277 --> 00:00:18.263
Ask Alex to explain the reason back in their own words.

00:00:18.503 --> 00:00:20.125
The likely gap is Awareness.

00:00:20.815 --> 00:00:23.375
Priya: “I understand the missed-handoff problem.

00:00:23.615 --> 00:00:32.084
I worry the queue will become a public ranking of who works fastest.” Priya has stated a reason for the change and a concern about participating.

00:00:32.324 --> 00:00:33.903
The likely gap is Desire.

00:00:34.143 --> 00:00:39.860
Acknowledge the concern, clarify verified data-use rules and invite specific questions.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:46.863
Do not promise that tracking will never be used for performance management unless that is actually approved and true.

00:00:47.553 --> 00:00:50.753
Awareness of a rationale does not guarantee willingness.

00:00:50.993 --> 00:00:53.638
Desire cannot be forced by repeating a slogan.

00:00:53.878 --> 00:00:57.505
An individual’s concerns may expose a real design issue.

00:00:57.745 --> 00:01:05.403
Sources for the element distinctions: a-r1 and a-r2; conversations and recommended responses are original.

00:01:06.093 --> 00:01:08.632
Practice a 45-second reply to Priya.

00:01:08.872 --> 00:01:12.179
Include a neutral question and one fact you would verify.

00:01:12.419 --> 00:01:14.936
Avoid assuming that reluctance means laziness.

00:01:15.626 --> 00:01:16.714
Now it's your turn.

00:01:16.954 --> 00:01:18.981
Priya raises the ranking concern.

00:01:19.221 --> 00:01:20.501
What do you say first?

00:01:20.741 --> 00:01:23.855
